\DOC DISCARD_STATE_TAC

\TYPE {DISCARD_STATE_TAC : string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Deletes from the assumption list every hypothesis that mentions the machine
state named by the given string, leaving the conclusion untouched.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {s : string} names a state variable; every assumption whose
statement involves that state is discarded, while assumptions not mentioning it
— arithmetic and bound facts, non-overlapping conditions, abbreviation
equations, and facts about other states — are kept in their original order.
{
       A ?- t
    ============ DISCARD_STATE_TAC s
       A' ?- t
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions deleted:
typically register, flag and memory {read} facts about the named state, the
{bytes_loaded} fact, the {MAYCHANGE} frame relating an earlier state to it, and
event-list facts about it.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces this single kind of outcome occurs, and the tactic is
always applied to goals that do have assumptions.

\ENDDOC
